{
  "abstract": "Cover sources of Weil classes are Hurwitz families of Galois covers of curves whose isotypic part of $H^1$, for an irreducible representation with imaginary quadratic character field $K$, is a $K$-Hodge structure of Weil type with Hodge numbers $(m,m)$ and a period map of dimension at least $m^2-1$. An earlier paper (hodge_conjecture_75) classified such sources when the primitive monodromy group contains a pseudo-reflection and left the reflection-free case open. We exclude several reflection-free classes by one method. The parabolic-cohomology budget forces at least $m^2+2$ branch points on $\\mathbb{P}^1$ and total rank $2u'+2m$. Riemann-Hurwitz and Scott's inequality give further constraints that are linear in the number of branch points of each class. A rational dual vector of the resulting linear program whose value exceeds the budget certifies that no source exists. Proved: for $n\\geq 9$ and every $m\\geq 3$, no scalar twist of the Specht modules $S^{(n-2,2)}$, $S^{(n-2,1,1)}$ or their conjugates is a reflection-free source; for $(n-2,2)$ and even $n$ the program forces $N'\\leq 8+(4m-12)/n$. Proved: normalisers of the Weil representations of $Sp_{2k}(q)$, $q$ odd, and $GU_n(q)$ with dimension at least 14, including outer automorphisms and with or without pseudo-reflections, are not sources for any $m\\geq 3$, apart from one case for $GU_n(2)$, $n\\geq 7$, which is conditional on a recalled realisation of the Weil module inside the normaliser of $2^{1+2n}$ and, for $n\\geq 8$, on $H^1(SU_n(2),\\mathbb{F}_4^n)=0$ (computed for $n\\leq 7$). Computational: all twisted Specht modules with $9\\leq n\\leq 17$ ($m=3,4$) and $18\\leq n\\leq 20$ (all $m$), and all 600 character-table-library pairs of degree 14 to 250 over a new imaginary quadratic field, for every $m\\geq 3$, with 1200 exact certificates and a standard-library checker. These are exclusions within stated classes. No case of the Hodge conjecture is proved.",
